# Runtime configuration for the nine Cloud Functions.
#
# Firebase reads `.env` and `.env.<projectId>` from THIS directory at deploy time and
# bakes the values into the deployed functions. Copy this to `.env.grow--line` and fill
# in the URL. That file is meant to be committed — it holds no secret, only a public
# address — which is why this example exists rather than a .gitignore entry.
#
# ## Why this file had to be created
#
# Seven of the nine functions do their work by calling this app's own HTTP routes, so
# the logic has one home rather than two. Every one of them starts with:
#
#     const base = process.env.SITE_URL;
#     if (!base || !secret) { console.error("... — not running"); return; }
#
# There was no `.env` in this directory and no other source for SITE_URL, so all seven
# would have deployed successfully, reported healthy, run on schedule, and returned
# immediately without doing anything — visible only to somebody reading Cloud Logging.
# The two that talk to Firestore directly (`onDailyLogWritten`, `purgeStaleHealthData`)
# were unaffected, which is what would have made the failure so hard to spot: the team
# roll-ups WOULD update, so the deploy would look like it worked.

# The public origin of the deployed app, no trailing slash. This is the App Hosting
# backend URL — the same value as NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL in apphosting.yaml.
#
#   SITE_URL=https://<backend-id>--grow--line.<region>.hosted.app
SITE_URL=

# NOT set here. CRON_SECRET is a Secret Manager secret, declared in each function's
# `secrets: ["CRON_SECRET"]` config, and it must EXIST BEFORE the first deploy — a
# secret reference that cannot resolve fails the whole deploy, not just that function.
#
#     firebase functions:secrets:set CRON_SECRET --project grow--line
#
# The routes fail closed without it, which is deliberate: these endpoints are reachable
# over the internet and a missing secret must mean "refuse", never "allow".
